# Persistent planning and review MVP — regrouping assessment

Assessment based on the persisted MVP and child success criteria, the current implementation, the live browser, and the recorded review feedback. These are proposed remaining requirements and priorities for discussion, not approved new implementation tasks. The top-level MVP remains Blocked and its acceptance review remains unfinished.

The persistence and shared operation foundation is useful, but the goal is a complete human-and-agent planning/review workflow. Repeatedly fixing individual annotations and passing tests has not yet established that complete workflow.

| Missing, incomplete, or needing refinement | Rationale |
| --- | --- |
| **Missing: complete relation and evidence editing.** Finish the relation modal, including attaching evidence and creating/linking nested tasks. | The live Relations add button is disabled, and the Attach shortcut was removed pending that modal. A user cannot independently build or revise the relationships that make a plan meaningful. This directly contradicts the complete contextual CRUD requirement; merely recording the modal in Backlog does not satisfy it. |
| **Broken: trustworthy file differences.** Correct the reported before/after comparison. | The incorrect file diff remains an explicitly recorded backlog bug. Reviewers must be able to distinguish what actually changed before deciding whether to accept it. A readable file viewer is insufficient if its change markings cannot be trusted. |
| **Incomplete: reliable file selection during review.** Resolve the recorded out-of-order response race. | The planning Changes implementation still awaits a file read and then renders its result without checking that this file is still selected. A slow earlier selection can replace a newer one. This risks reviewing or annotating the wrong file, so it is more than cosmetic refinement. |
| **Missing: node comparisons that preserve the node editor.** Compare typed fields and relations in their normal layout. | The current comparison path renders values and relations as text blocks. It does not meet the accepted no/inline/side-by-side node comparison requirement or your explicit instruction to retain the node editor layout. |
| **Needs refinement: annotations usable for an actual review.** Make capture, editing, screenshot association and agent handoff coherent. | You still choose Codex annotations because the screenshot stays associated with the issue and the feedback reaches the next prompt. Existing app annotation save/queue/chat paths are a foundation, but the current interaction has not won acceptance. The minimum is one reliable in-app route preserving visual context and intent; a wholesale annotation platform redesign is unnecessary for this MVP. |
| **Unproven: one traceable feedback lifecycle across Inputs, review and chat.** Keep the finding, discussion, correction and disposition connected. | The input owner explicitly distinguishes captures from review discussions, and the current acceptance review has no discussion threads despite the many review annotations. Shared storage alone does not establish an end-to-end workflow. We need to demonstrate that feedback survives each handoff and that unresolved acceptance blockers remain visible to the review gate. |
| **Incomplete: a clearly identified current acceptance candidate.** Align the presented app, submitted changes, preview and check evidence. | R03 still targets revision 50, submitted on 27 September, and its recorded passing run also targets 50; the live plan was at 120 before this assessment. Changes reads immutable submitted evidence while Preview uses the configured application preview. Later fixes and their separate successful tests must not be mistaken for acceptance evidence for an older snapshot. Establish and identify the candidate explicitly while retaining prior history. |
| **Missing/unverified: complete human/tool operation parity.** Audit every promised operation, including planning import/export. | The CRUD task promises a discoverable human path for every supported planning operation, and the integration criteria explicitly include import/export. The current planning transfer API exposes attaching/detaching evidence, and the planning UI provides no planning import/export path. Existing session/archive features do not by themselves demonstrate planning graph round trips. Verify links, priorities, deletion constraints and history in the same audit. |
| **Incomplete: contributor integration through the running app.** Demonstrate a contributor-defined type, field and renderer without editing the planning screen. | Engine type validation and UI renderer registries exist and are tested, but the active planning schema, node-view list and renderer composition are still assembled explicitly in planning code. A registry seam is not yet evidence that a contributor can extend the delivered application. The previously deferred idea of unifying node-view and field-renderer discovery remains separate. |
| **Needs refinement: actionable lifecycle state and navigation.** Explain why an item is Blocked or frozen and expose its relevant review and allowed next actions. | The top-level blocking invariant is now enforced, but finding the reason still requires interpreting graph/relations. The node editor also determines editability locally without accounting for every active target freeze, so some prohibited edits are only rejected on Save. Valid state should be understandable before the user attempts an invalid operation. |
| **Incomplete: truthful completion status and the real acceptance walkthrough.** Reconcile child status with these gaps, then finish the user-led R03 scenario. | “Compose complete UI CRUD and review controls” and “Connect real evidence and review gates” are marked completed despite gaps against their own criteria. Integration is still Running and R03 is submitted without an outcome. Demonstrate capture → plan/link/prioritize → inspect evidence → review/comment → correction → review again, with UI/MCP readback and restart, then obtain your explicit acceptance of that candidate. Passing component tests cannot supply that acceptance. |

Recommended sequence: restore trustworthy inspection and relationship/evidence editing; complete feedback and review-iteration continuity; then perform the operation-parity and user acceptance walkthrough. Reassess completion statuses against that evidence instead of treating essential acceptance gaps as unrelated future refinements.

Keep these explicitly deferred: changes beyond persisted files, a unified discovery framework, Markdown as a semantic type, shared cross-repository check infrastructure, and duration/cost features. They are not needed merely to declare this MVP complete.

The annotation fix in this pass requires a non-empty string comment in field declarations. The engine checks factory declarations, raw contributor registration and value validation; the shared editor uses the authoritative comment for the field-name tooltip, value tooltip and accessible description. Existing application/template declarations have meaningful comments. A non-empty check cannot prove the explanation is useful, so comment quality remains part of contributor review.

Source trail: the persisted top-level MVP; “Compose complete UI CRUD and review controls”; “Connect real evidence and review gates”; “Integrate the surfaces and complete the usage walkthrough”; R03 target/runs/discussions; recorded annotation, diff and relation-modal inputs/tasks; current `app/public/planning.js`, `app/planning-files.mjs`, `app/planning-transfer.mjs`, `app/planning-inputs.mjs`, and `app/planning-contract.mjs`. No review outcome or existing task status was changed to make this assessment look complete.
